<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lee Teng-hui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lee Teng-hui was the first Taiwan-born president of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, succeeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-ching-kuo">Chiang Ching-kuo</a> in 1988 and serving until 2000. An agricultural economist, he led the transition to democracy, abolishing the Temporary Provisions in 1991, holding fully elected legislatures and in 1996 winning the first direct presidential election, despite Chinese missile tests during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-taiwan-strait-crisis">Third Taiwan Strait Crisis</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lee-teng-hui.jpg" alt="Lee Teng-hui" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lee Teng-hui Foundation · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lee_Teng-hui_official_portrait.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lee Teng-hui was born in 1923 in the farming community of Sanshi Village, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamsui">Tamsui</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> descent. His father, a policeman, worked for the colonial Japanese government and owned land. In 1943 he passed the competitive entrance exam for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and enrolled in the Faculty of Agriculture at Kyoto Imperial University, where he was interested in Marxian economics, and he served in the Imperial Japanese Army in the war. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in March 1946, he enrolled at National Taiwan University, where he briefly joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> twice, in 1946 and 1947, and withdrew each time. He graduated in 1949, took a master's degree at Iowa State University in 1953 and a doctorate in agricultural economics at Cornell University in 1968.</p><p>By 1970 Lee was a well-known agricultural economist at National Taiwan University and the Joint Commission on Rural Reconstruction. Through the KMT official Wang Tso-jung he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-ching-kuo">Chiang Ching-kuo</a>, who was impressed by him, and Lee joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> in July 1970. When Chiang became premier in 1972 he made Lee, at 49, minister without portfolio responsible for agricultural development, his youngest cabinet minister. Lee was mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a> from 1978 and governor of Taiwan Province from 1981.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-ching-kuo">Chiang Ching-kuo</a> died in January 1988, and Lee succeeded him as president. Within the party, figures of the Palace Faction, among them General Hau Pei-tsun and Soong Mei-ling, doubted his leadership, but with the help of James Soong, who urged that each day of delay was a day of disrespect to Ching-kuo, he became party chairman. At the 13th National Congress in July 1988 he named 31 Central Committee members, 16 of them benshengren, locally born Taiwanese, so that for the first time they held a majority. He ordered the release of General Sun Li-jen from 33 years of house arrest.</p><p>Lee was the first president born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and the first to be directly elected, and the last to be indirectly elected. He supported the Taiwanese localization movement and sought greater international recognition for Taiwan. In 1999 the foreign ministry, under Lee, asserted that the whole of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> and the Spratly islands belonged to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He left office in 2000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After leaving office Lee supported the pro-independence Taiwan Solidarity Union, and was expelled from the KMT on 21 September 2001 after endorsing its candidates. He supported changing the name of the country to the Republic of Taiwan and kept warm ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. He was a Presbyterian and was baptised in 1961. Lee died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a> on 30 July 2020. Supporters called him Mr. Democracy and the father of Taiwan's democracy, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> figures held him responsible for breaking up the party-state system.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lee Teng-hui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Lee Teng-hui</b> the Temporary Provisions were abolished in 1991, the legislature was fully re-elected in 1992 and the president was directly elected from 1996, completing a peaceful transition to democracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sanshi village, in what is now <a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. Died in Taipei Veterans General Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.24,121.53&amp;year=1986" aria-label="Sanshi village on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7771 1235 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lee Teng-hui"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8221 1505h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8221 1505h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.24,121.53&amp;year=1986">What was here in 1986? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
